  - id: C-MIG-0029
    path: src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_extern/math.rs
    symbols:
      - rt_math_sqrt
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.math.special.sqrt_f64 (already implemented in
      src/lib/common/math/special.spl:421-427, delegating to the private
      Newton's-method kernel `_sqrt_f64` at special.spl:73-83 -- no new lib
      file was written this round; the pre-existing kernel already satisfies
      the "independent of the extern" bar, per that module's own header
      comment stating it deliberately avoids the rt_math_* SFFI externs)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_math_sqrt(x: f64) -> f64 is registered in the interpreter's extern
      dispatch table (interpreter_extern/mod.rs:1580,
      math::rt_math_sqrt_fn) via Rust's hardware `f64::sqrt` -- a
      correctly-rounded IEEE-754 sqrt instruction. No C runtime counterpart
      exists (`grep -rn rt_math_sqrt src/runtime/*.c src/runtime/*.h`
      returns zero hits); this is a pure-Rust-interpreter extern, unlike
      C-MIG-0027/0028 which had a `runtime_native.c` implementation behind
      the same-named extern. `sqrt_f64` (special.spl) is 40 iterations of
      Newton's method against the same contract (x >= 0), genuinely
      independent of the hardware instruction.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/math/special_sqrt_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "6 examples, 6 passed (KAT vectors incl. perfect
      squares, zero, tolerance-based irrational-result comparison
      (Newton's-method vs hardware sqrt can disagree by a few ULPs -- not
      bit-exact, unlike the integer/text C-MIG-0027/0028 oracles), an
      EXPLICIT SEMANTIC-DIVERGENCE assertion for negative input (oracle
      correctly returns NaN per IEEE-754; sqrt_f64 is undocumented/unguarded
      for x < 0 per its own docstring and returns finite garbage instead --
      verified live: sqrt_f64(-4.0) == 6.070281514079917, NOT NaN), single-bit
      corruption discrimination, determinism check, 100-vector shared-logic
      bulk differential loop restricted to the documented non-negative
      domain with forced perfect-square boundary values at fixed moduli,
      2026-08-18)"
    c_status: no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING (algorithmic fix applied, gap narrowed but not
      closed) — 2026-08-18 update: `_sqrt_f64` rewritten from a fixed
      40-iteration Newton loop starting at g=x to a range-reduced
      ([1,4) via divide/multiply-by-4) start plus a relative-epsilon
      convergence test capped at 8 iterations. Ratio improved from ~17.7x to
      ~12.2x over the same 100-vector bulk corpus (simple_us=5969, c_us=488,
      single run, tree-walk interpreter via `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18) --
      still well above the plan's 2x threshold. Iteration count dropped from
      a fixed 40 to a data-dependent 1-8 (typically 5-6 to converge), so the
      remaining ~12x is dominated by interpreter per-iteration/per-call
      dispatch overhead, not algorithmic iteration count -- this is an
      interpreter tax, not a further algorithmic defect in this kernel. See
      doc/08_tracking/bug/codegen_lane_still_slow_base64url_utf8_time_utils_2026-08-18.md."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering x values (seeded LCG cycling through
      0.0..1000.0 in 0.001 steps, plus forced boundary values at fixed
      moduli: 0.0 at i % 17 == 0, 1.0 at i % 13 == 0, 144.0 at i % 11 == 0,
      0.25 at i % 7 == 0), 2026-08-18 (pre-fix): simple_us=8109 c_us=457,
      ratio ~17.7x. 2026-08-18 (post seeded-guess+convergence-test fix):
      simple_us=5969 c_us=488, ratio ~12.2x.
    shared_corpus: 100
    perf_codegen: "OPEN — codegen (Cranelift JIT) lane re-measured 2026-08-18,
      binary bin/release/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple (59673480 bytes,
      mtime 2026-08-18 06:12:48). Harness: bench_sqrt_f64_codegen.spl
      (scratchpad, not committed), 100-vector pinned seeded corpus spanning
      1e-300..1e300 (LCG-generated exponent+mantissa) plus exact-power/
      boundary cases (0.0, 1.0, 4.0, 9.0, 16.0, 1e-300, 1e300, 0.999999,
      1.000001, 1e6); KAT OK 100/100 on both engines before timing.
      SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=interpreter/jit selects the engine (SIMPLE_NO_JIT
      and SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT under bare `bin/simple run` are no-ops here since
      `run` already Cranelift-JITs by default -- confirmed by identical
      numbers with/without SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT=1; see .claude/rules/testing.md).
      200 reps x 100 vectors, batch-timed (one clock pair around the whole
      loop): interpreter simple_us=9230087 c_us=59705 ratio=154.6x; JIT
      simple_us=35653 c_us=577 ratio=61.8x. Per-call-probe (clock read around
      every call, shown for probe-distortion comparison per the time_utils
      precedent): interpreter ratio=106.9x; JIT ratio=36.0x. JIT is ~2.5x
      faster than the interpreter in wall time (9.23M us -> 35.7K us for the
      Simple side) and the ratio-vs-oracle improves accordingly (154.6x ->
      61.8x), confirming the residual gap is partly interpreter dispatch tax
      as hypothesized -- but codegen is still 61.8x the oracle, nowhere near
      the <=2x threshold, so the claim that codegen alone would close the gap
      is NOT supported. Remaining OPEN; the oracle call itself is ~0.03us/call
      (hardware instruction), a bar this Newton-iteration kernel (needing
      1-8 iterations of several f64 ops each) cannot approach even fully
      JIT-compiled. See doc/08_tracking/bug/codegen_lane_still_slow_base64url_utf8_time_utils_2026-08-18.md
      (addendum, 2026-08-18)."

  - id: C-MIG-0030
    path: src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_extern/math.rs
    symbols:
      - rt_math_cbrt
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.math.cbrt.cbrt_f64 (NEW file src/lib/common/math/cbrt.spl
      written this round -- deliberately NOT written into special.spl, which
      is this round's HARD AVOID because another session is active there.
      80-iteration Newton's-method kernel starting from g=1.0, genuinely
      independent of the rt_math_cbrt extern; the existing math_cbrt in
      math.spl was rejected as a candidate because it just calls
      rt_math_cbrt directly and is therefore not independent)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_math_cbrt(x: f64) -> f64 is registered in the interpreter's extern
      dispatch table (interpreter_extern/mod.rs:1563, math::rt_math_cbrt_fn).
      No C runtime counterpart exists (`grep -rn rt_math_cbrt src/runtime/*.c
      src/runtime/*.h` returns zero hits); this is a pure-Rust-interpreter
      extern, same kind as C-MIG-0029's rt_math_sqrt (no_c_implementation_
      rust_only_extern), unlike C-MIG-0027/0028's runtime_native.c-backed
      externs. Unlike sqrt, cube root is defined for all real x (including
      negative), so cbrt_f64 has no domain restriction the oracle also lacks
      -- handled via the standard odd-function reflection
      cbrt(-a) = -cbrt(a). Bug found and fixed while writing this spec: the
      initial kernel started Newton's method at g = x (mirroring
      _sqrt_f64's g = x start) and converged far too slowly for x << 1 --
      cbrt_f64(1.0e-8) returned 0.0021988... instead of the correct
      0.0021544..., a ~2% error, from 60 iterations starting ~2e5x away
      from the root. Fixed by starting at the fixed point g = 1.0 instead
      and raising to 80 iterations; re-verified against the oracle across
      the full KAT + tolerance-vector + 100-vector bulk-loop suite below,
      including the exact 1.0e-8/1.0e10 values that exposed the bug.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/math/cbrt_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "7 examples, 7 passed (KAT vectors incl. positive AND
      negative perfect cubes, zero, tolerance-based irrational-result
      comparison, domain-boundary coverage (+-0.0, +-infinity, NaN input --
      all agree with the oracle, no semantic divergence found unlike
      C-MIG-0029's sqrt_f64), single-bit corruption discrimination,
      determinism check, 100-vector shared-logic bulk differential loop
      covering the FULL domain (positive and negative, unlike sqrt_f64's
      NaN-producing negative branch which needed a separate loop) with
      forced perfect-cube and zero/negative-zero boundary values at fixed
      moduli, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING (algorithmic fix applied, gap narrowed but not
      closed) — 2026-08-18 update: `_cbrt_pos` rewritten from a fixed
      80-iteration Newton loop starting at g=1.0 to a range-reduced
      ([1,8) via divide/multiply-by-8) start plus a relative-epsilon
      convergence test capped at 8 iterations. Ratio improved from ~34.9x to
      ~13.5x over the same 100-vector bulk corpus (simple_us=6320, c_us=467,
      single run, tree-walk interpreter via `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18) --
      still well above the plan's 2x threshold. Iteration count dropped from
      a fixed 80 to a data-dependent 1-8 (typically 5-6 to converge), so the
      remaining ~13.5x is dominated by interpreter per-iteration/per-call
      dispatch overhead, not algorithmic iteration count -- this is an
      interpreter tax, not a further algorithmic defect in this kernel. See
      doc/08_tracking/bug/codegen_lane_still_slow_base64url_utf8_time_utils_2026-08-18.md."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering x values (seeded LCG, magnitude range
      sub-1 to large, both signs, plus forced boundary values at fixed
      moduli: 0.0 at i % 19 == 0, -0.0 at i % 17 == 0, 1.0 at i % 13 == 0,
      125.0 at i % 11 == 0, -0.125 at i % 7 == 0, sign-flip at i % 5 == 0),
      2026-08-18 (pre-fix): simple_us=17390 c_us=498, ratio ~34.9x.
      2026-08-18 (post seeded-guess+convergence-test fix): simple_us=6320
      c_us=467, ratio ~13.5x.
    shared_corpus: 100
    perf_codegen: "OPEN — codegen (Cranelift JIT) lane re-measured 2026-08-18,
      binary bin/release/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple (59673480 bytes,
      mtime 2026-08-18 06:12:48). Harness: bench_cbrt_f64_codegen.spl
      (scratchpad, not committed), same 100-vector pinned corpus generator as
      C-MIG-0029's sqrt harness (1e-300..1e300 span plus exact-power/boundary
      cases); KAT OK 100/100 on both engines before timing. Found and worked
      around a genuine JIT correctness pitfall while writing this harness:
      `if not _approx(f(x), g(x), tol):` with two function-call results passed
      directly as call arguments produced a wrong boolean under
      SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=jit (false KAT failure on values that print
      identically), while binding each call to a `val` first and passing the
      locals resolved it -- not filed as a separate bug doc since it did not
      block this measurement, but noted here since it would silently corrupt
      any codegen-lane harness using the same call-nesting pattern.
      SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=interpreter/jit selects the engine (bare
      `bin/simple run` already Cranelift-JITs by default; SIMPLE_JIT_STRICT=1
      is a no-op on top of it here). 200 reps x 100 vectors, batch-timed:
      interpreter simple_us=6688979 c_us=62860 ratio=106.4x; JIT
      simple_us=24911 c_us=940 ratio=26.5x. Per-call-probe: interpreter
      ratio=75.0x; JIT ratio=17.0x. JIT is ~2.5x faster than interpreter in
      wall time (6.69M us -> 24.9K us) and the oracle ratio improves
      accordingly (106.4x -> 26.5x) -- codegen IS materially better, as
      hypothesized, but 26.5x is still far above the <=2x threshold, so the
      claim that codegen alone closes the gap is NOT supported for cbrt_f64
      either. Remaining OPEN. See
      doc/08_tracking/bug/codegen_lane_still_slow_base64url_utf8_time_utils_2026-08-18.md
      (addendum, 2026-08-18)."

