    symbols:
      - rt_path_ext
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.path_pure.path_ext (NEW file
      src/lib/common/path_pure.spl -- uncontested. Scans the final path
      component by hand for the last separator and the last dot, an
      explicit character-scan approach, not a re-wrap of Rust's `Path`
      type machinery)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_path_ext(path: text) -> text is registered in the interpreter's
      extern dispatch table (interpreter_extern/mod.rs:1607,
      file_io::rt_path_ext) via Rust's `std::path::Path::extension()`. No C
      runtime counterpart exists (`grep -rn rt_path_ext src/runtime/*.c
      src/runtime/*.h` returns zero hits; this project's runtime C sources
      have no `rt_path_*` symbols at all -- path parsing is Rust-`std`-only
      here); this is a pure-Rust-interpreter extern, same kind as
      C-MIG-0029..0035 (no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern). Rejected
      candidates: `rt_path_basename` / `rt_path_dirname` (mod.rs:1605/1606)
      are registered legitimate siblings, but `extension()` was picked for
      its sharper edge-case surface (dotfiles, trailing dots, no-dot
      names); `rt_path_absolute` / `rt_path_relative` (mod.rs:1604/1609)
      are registered but touch the real filesystem/CWD, which would make a
      pure differential spec environment-dependent -- deferred.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/path_pure_ext_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "5 examples, 5 passed (KAT vectors incl. simple/
      nested/absolute paths with extensions; edge cases: empty string,
      single char with no dot, no-extension name, dotfile with no further
      dot, dotfile WITH a further dot, '.' and '..', trailing separator on
      a name with no dot AND on a name with a dot ('a.b/'), path that is
      only separators ('/'), repeated separators before the final
      component, trailing dot with nothing after it, bare filename with no
      separator, dot as first char of a non-leading directory component;
      single-char-flip discrimination; determinism check; 100-vector
      shared-logic bulk differential loop mixing leading directories,
      trailing separators, and repeated separators over 9 base-name
      shapes, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple path_ext measured ~10.7x the
      Rust `Path::extension()` oracle over the 100-vector bulk corpus
      (simple_us=5688, c_us=532, single run, tree-walk interpreter via
      `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18), above the plan's 2x threshold.
      Recorded honestly, not hidden -- same interpreter-lane caveat as
      C-MIG-0035 and prior PERF findings (character-by-character text
      scanning plus `substring` calls under the tree-walk interpreter vs a
      single Rust `Path` parse)."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering path strings, 2026-08-18: simple_us=5688
      c_us=532, ratio ~10.7x.
    shared_corpus: 100
    fixed_perf_bugs: []
    domain_bug_note: >
      REAL DEFECT FOUND AND FIXED via the 100-vector bulk loop (the
      hand-picked KATs alone did not catch it): the first version of
      `last_component` in src/lib/common/path_pure.spl returned "" for ANY
      path ending in a separator, on the wrong assumption that
      `Path::file_name()` is None once a path ends in "/". Direct
      comparison against `rt_path_ext` proved otherwise -- Rust's
      `Path::file_name()`/`Path::extension()` ignore trailing separators
      and use the component before them: "a.b/" -> extension "b" and
      "dir/f.rs/" -> extension "rs" on the oracle, both nonempty, while the
      buggy Simple side returned "". Fixed by stripping trailing
      separators before locating the final component (a path that becomes
      fully empty after stripping, e.g. "/", still correctly yields "").
      Re-verified against the oracle for "a.b/", "dir/f.rs/", and "/".

  - id: C-MIG-0037
    path: src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_extern/file_io.rs
    symbols:
      - rt_path_basename
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.path_pure.path_basename (extends the EXISTING
      src/lib/common/path_pure.spl created by C-MIG-0036. Byte-consistent
      left-to-right separator scan plus a "." / ".." check on the final
      component, an explicit character-scan approach, not a re-wrap of
      Rust's `Path` type machinery)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_path_basename(path: text) -> text is registered in the
      interpreter's extern dispatch table (interpreter_extern/mod.rs:1605,
      file_io::rt_path_basename) via Rust's `std::path::Path::file_name()`.
      No C runtime counterpart exists (`grep -rn rt_path_basename
      src/runtime/*.c src/runtime/*.h` returns zero hits); pure-Rust-
      interpreter extern, same kind as C-MIG-0029..0036
      (no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern). Pre-approved and
      deprioritized by C-MIG-0036 in favor of `path_ext`; taken up this
      round together with its sibling `rt_path_dirname` (C-MIG-0038). No
      new rejects this round.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/path_pure_basename_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "5 examples, 5 passed (KAT vectors; mandated edge
      table: empty string, '/', '//', bare name, trailing separator(s),
      repeated separators, leading '/', leading './'/'../',  bare '.'/'..',
      multibyte UTF-8 segment, Windows-style backslash path;
      single-char-flip discrimination; determinism check; 100-vector
      shared-logic bulk differential loop over 7 base segment shapes x 3
      separator styles with leading/trailing/bare variants, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple path_basename measured
      ~14.6x the Rust `Path::file_name()` oracle over the 100-vector bulk
      corpus (simple_us=7731, c_us=530, single run, tree-walk interpreter
      via `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18), above the plan's 2x threshold.
      Recorded honestly, not hidden -- same interpreter-lane caveat as
      C-MIG-0035/0036 and prior PERF findings."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering path strings, 2026-08-18: simple_us=7731
      c_us=530, ratio ~14.6x.
    shared_corpus: 100
    fixed_perf_bugs: []
    domain_bug_note: >
      REAL DEFECT FOUND AND FIXED via the multibyte-UTF-8 edge probe
      (neither the hand-picked KATs nor the bulk loop's random seeding
      alone hit this until a directed "café/x" / "dir/café" probe was
      added): `text.len()`/`.substring()` are BYTE-indexed while
      `.char_at()` is CODEPOINT-indexed, and the two are NOT
      interchangeable. Scanning with `.char_at()` and then slicing the
      SAME index with `.substring()` corrupts multibyte input -- for
      "dir/café" (9 bytes, 8 codepoints), `.char_at()`-scanning finds "é"
      at codepoint index 7, but `path.substring(4, 8)` (byte offsets) then
      cuts the 2-byte "é" in half, yielding "caf<invalid-byte>" instead of
      "café". Fixed by scanning and slicing `path_basename`/`path_dirname`
      using ONLY byte-consistent operations throughout (`.len()` for the
      bound, `.substring(i, i+1) == "/"` instead of `.char_at(i) == "/"`
      for the separator test -- safe because "/" is single-byte ASCII).
      SCOPE LIMITATION (documented, not a bug): a "." component that is
      NOT the path's very first component is silently elided by Rust's
      `Path::components()` (e.g. "dir/." basenames to "dir", not ""); this
      implementation does not replicate that normalization and the bulk
      loop's segment generator deliberately excludes "." to stay in-scope
      (confirmed directly, see spec comment). `path_ext`'s pre-existing
      `last_component` (C-MIG-0036) still has the byte/codepoint mixing
      bug for multibyte input -- left as a follow-up, not modified here to
      avoid touching a concurrently-edited shared file.

  - id: C-MIG-0038
    path: src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_extern/file_io.rs
    symbols:
      - rt_path_dirname
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.path_pure.path_dirname (extends the EXISTING
      src/lib/common/path_pure.spl, companion to C-MIG-0037's
      path_basename in the same file/commit. Byte-consistent separator
      scan producing the parent-component substring, an explicit
      character-scan approach, not a re-wrap of Rust's `Path` type
      machinery)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_path_dirname(path: text) -> text is registered in the
      interpreter's extern dispatch table (interpreter_extern/mod.rs:1606,
      file_io::rt_path_dirname) via Rust's `std::path::Path::parent()`. No
      C runtime counterpart exists (`grep -rn rt_path_dirname
      src/runtime/*.c src/runtime/*.h` returns zero hits); pure-Rust-
      interpreter extern, same kind as C-MIG-0029..0037
      (no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern). Pre-approved and
      deprioritized by C-MIG-0036, taken up this round paired with
      `rt_path_basename` (C-MIG-0037). No new rejects this round.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/path_pure_dirname_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "5 examples, 5 passed (KAT vectors; mandated edge
      table: empty string, '/', '//', bare name, trailing separator(s),
      repeated separators, leading '/' (incl. repeated), leading
      './'/'../', bare '.'/'..', multibyte UTF-8 segment, Windows-style
      backslash path; single-char-flip discrimination; determinism check;
      100-vector shared-logic bulk differential loop over 7 base segment
      shapes x 3 separator styles with leading/trailing/bare variants,
      2026-08-18)"
    c_status: no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple path_dirname measured ~14.8x
      the Rust `Path::parent()` oracle over the 100-vector bulk corpus
      (simple_us=8185, c_us=554, single run, tree-walk interpreter via
      `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18), above the plan's 2x threshold.
      Recorded honestly, not hidden -- same interpreter-lane caveat as
      C-MIG-0035/0036/0037 and prior PERF findings."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering path strings, 2026-08-18: simple_us=8185
      c_us=554, ratio ~14.8x.
    shared_corpus: 100
    fixed_perf_bugs: []
    domain_bug_note: >
      Shares the byte-vs-codepoint-indexing defect fix with its
      C-MIG-0037 companion `path_basename` (same commit, same
      `is_sep_at` helper) -- see that entry's domain_bug_note for the
      mechanism and the "café/x"/"dir/café" repro. No additional defect
      specific to `path_dirname` was found beyond that shared fix and the
      same documented non-leading-"." elision scope limitation (bulk
      generator excludes "." for the same reason as C-MIG-0037).

  - id: C-MIG-0039
    path: src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/interpreter_extern/simd.rs
    symbols:
      - rt_text_is_ascii
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.ascii_pure.is_ascii_text (NEW file
      src/lib/common/ascii_pure.spl -- uncontested, outside all HARD-AVOID
      paths for this round including text_ascii.spl and string_core.spl.
      Scans the raw byte array by hand (`s.bytes()`, per-byte `>= 128`
      check), genuinely different machinery from the extern's Rust
      stdlib `str::is_ascii()` call)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_text_is_ascii(s: text) -> bool is registered in the interpreter's
      extern dispatch table (interpreter_extern/mod.rs:1916,
      simd::rt_text_is_ascii). No C runtime counterpart exists (`grep -rn
      rt_text_is_ascii src/runtime/*.c src/runtime/*.h` returns zero
      hits, verified before writing the spec); this is a pure-Rust-
      interpreter extern, same kind as C-MIG-0029..0038
      (no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern). Rejected candidates:
      `rt_text_validate_utf8` / `rt_text_find_invalid_utf8`
      (mod.rs:1915, simd.rs:2044/2054) already covered by C-MIG-0022;
      `rt_path_basename` / `rt_path_dirname` reserved for concurrently-
      landing C-MIG-0037/0038 per this round's HARD AVOID list. No
      production `extern fn rt_text_is_ascii` caller migration was
      performed -- this entry only establishes differential correctness +
      perf evidence.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/ascii_pure_ext_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "5 examples, 5 passed (KAT vectors incl. ASCII and
      multibyte UTF-8 (café, 日本語); edge cases: empty string, NUL (0x00),
      DEL (0x7f), first codepoint past the ASCII boundary (U+0080),
      non-ASCII at string start/end, all-ASCII-whitespace, 4-byte emoji
      codepoint, long all-ASCII run; single-char-flip discrimination;
      determinism check; 100-vector shared-logic bulk differential loop
      over ASCII fragments with non-ASCII prepended/appended/absent,
      2026-08-18)"
    c_status: no_c_implementation_rust_only_extern
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple is_ascii_text measured ~4.15x
      the Rust-interpreter oracle over the 100-vector bulk corpus
      (simple_us=2024, c_us=488, single run, tree-walk interpreter via
      `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18). Above the plan's 2x threshold;
      recorded honestly, not hidden -- same interpreter-lane caveat as
      prior PERF findings (bin/simple test is not the engine ordinary
      programs run on). Not fixed here: the Simple side is already a
      tight byte-scan with early-exit on the first non-ASCII byte, so the
      residual gap is per-call/array-indexing interpreter dispatch
      overhead rather than an algorithmic issue; not filed as a
      standalone bug given the small absolute magnitude (~1.5us/call).
      If the Simple side accumulates text in a loop, note: it does not
      here — is_ascii_text never builds a text, only compares byte values,
      so the O(n^2) `text + text` concat pattern documented in
      text_builder.spl does not apply."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering text vectors (ASCII fragments combined
      with non-ASCII prepend/append/none via seeded LCG), 2026-08-18:
      simple_us=2024 c_us=488, ratio ~4.15x.
    shared_corpus: 100

  - id: C-MIG-0040
    duplicate_target_of: C-MIG-0025
    duplicate_note: >-
      REVIEW CORRECTION 2026-08-18. This targets rt_string_ends_with, ALREADY
      covered by C-MIG-0025 (std.common.string_core.str_ends_with, spec
      test/01_unit/lib/common/string_core_ends_with_crosslang_spec.spl, 6/6).
      Do NOT count 0040 as new symbol coverage -- the covered-symbol total is
      unchanged by it. Retained rather than deleted because it has independent
      value: a SECOND, byte-array-based implementation (suffix_pure.ends_with_text)
      differentially checked against the same oracle with 10 edge cases, which
      is a stronger agreement signal than either alone. Treat it as a
      cross-implementation check, not a migration.
    path: src/runtime/runtime_native.c
    symbols:
      - rt_string_ends_with
    classification: product_algorithm
    replacement: std.common.suffix_pure.ends_with_text (NEW file
      src/lib/common/suffix_pure.spl -- uncontested, outside all
      HARD-AVOID paths including string_core.spl, which already has its
      own `str_ends_with`. Compares raw byte arrays (`s.bytes()` /
      `suffix.bytes()`) tail-first via an explicit byte-index loop,
      genuinely different machinery from either the C or the
      Rust-interpreter implementation)
    simple_callers_migrated: []
    caller_note: >
      rt_string_ends_with(s: text, suffix: text) -> bool is declared
      extern in src/lib/text.spl, with a real C definition in
      src/runtime/runtime_native.c and matching declaration in
      runtime.h (`grep -rn rt_string_ends_with src/runtime/*.c
      src/runtime/*.h` returns hits in both, verified before writing the
      spec) -- C-backed, unlike C-MIG-0039's Rust-interpreter-only
      sibling. The interpreter-lane oracle used by the differential spec
      is the Rust-interpreter's own extern registration
      (interpreter_extern/sffi_string.rs::rt_string_ends_with_fn,
      registered mod.rs:1889, `s.as_bytes().ends_with(suffix.as_bytes())`)
      which mirrors the C semantics. Rejected candidates: `rt_string_rfind`
      (registered alongside rt_string_ends_with_fn) already covered by
      C-MIG-0024; `rt_dict_contains` (mod.rs:1171) deferred to keep this
      round's two picks both in the text domain. No production
      `extern fn rt_string_ends_with` caller migration was performed --
      this entry only establishes differential correctness + perf
      evidence.
    correctness_spec: test/01_unit/lib/common/suffix_pure_ext_crosslang_spec.spl
    correctness_result: "5 examples, 5 passed (KAT vectors incl. ordinary
      filename/extension suffixes and multi-segment suffixes; edge cases:
      empty subject and/or suffix, suffix longer than subject, suffix
      equal to subject, single-char match/mismatch, suffix matching a
      middle substring but not the tail, multibyte UTF-8 suffix
      match/mismatch, all-whitespace, NUL (0x00) and DEL (0x7f) byte
      boundaries; single-char-flip discrimination; determinism check;
      100-vector shared-logic bulk differential loop over 8x8 subject x
      suffix combinations incl. multibyte, 2026-08-18)"
    c_status: retained_as_test_oracle
    perf_status: "PERF FINDING — pure-Simple ends_with_text measured
      ~4.45x the C-backed oracle over the 100-vector bulk corpus
      (simple_us=2288, c_us=514, single run, tree-walk interpreter via
      `bin/simple test`, 2026-08-18). Above the plan's 2x threshold;
      recorded honestly, not hidden -- same interpreter-lane caveat as
      prior PERF findings. Not fixed here: the Simple side is already a
      tight tail-comparison byte loop with early-exit and no
      allocation, so the residual gap is per-call/array-indexing
      interpreter dispatch overhead; not filed as a standalone bug given
      the small absolute magnitude (~1.8us/call). No text accumulation
      in a loop occurs here (ends_with_text never builds a text), so the
      O(n^2) `text + text` concat pattern documented in text_builder.spl
      does not apply."
    perf_measured: >
      100 shared branch-covering text vectors (8 subjects x 8 suffixes,
      incl. multibyte UTF-8), 2026-08-18: simple_us=2288 c_us=514,
      ratio ~4.45x.
    shared_corpus: 100
